# The three-valued X = Y of intervals with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One enclosure
# interface", "Comparisons"): yes when every member of X equals every member of Y, that is X = Y =
# [v, v], x_l = x_u = y_l = y_u; no when no member of X equals one of Y, the intervals disjoint, x_u
# < y_l or y_u < x_l; both false otherwise and when an operand is empty (the yes of the empty set
# fails by itself: its bounds differ). By the comparisons of T. Exact. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_equal_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
input:         stored yl
input:         stored yu
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      interval yl yu
ensures:       trit yes no eq x y x=xl,xu y=yl,yu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the bounds compared, and a test of emptiness (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical operand

ea  = eq(xl, xu)
eb  = eq(xu, yl)
ec  = eq(yl, yu)
eab = and(ea, eb)
yes = and(eab, ec)
nx  = lt(xl, const inf)
ny  = lt(yl, const inf)
ne  = and(nx, ny)
da  = lt(xu, yl)
db  = lt(yu, xl)
dd  = or(da, db)
no  = and(dd, ne)
